<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Augustus, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a>–<a href="/years/1828">1828</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Karl August of Saxe-Weimar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Augustus, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>, ruled the duchy from 1775, first under his mother Anna Amalia's guidance. He brought Goethe to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> as friend and minister, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> and Schiller followed, making the small court the centre of German classicism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> raised him to grand duke, and in 1816 he granted one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s first constitutions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-augustus-grand-duke-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach.jpg" alt="Charles Augustus, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Christoph Kolbe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gro%C3%9Fherzog_Carl_August_1822.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Karl August, also called Charles Augustus, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> on 3 September 1757, the eldest son of Duke Ernst August II and Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-amalia-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Anna Amalia of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</a>. His father died when he was nine months old, and his mother supervised his upbringing as regent. Count Johann Eustach von Görtz was his governor, and in 1771 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-martin-wieland">Christoph Martin Wieland</a> became his tutor. On a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1774 he met the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> through the poet Karl Ludwig von Knebel. After he returned to Weimar and took over the government, he married Luise Auguste of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> on 3 October 1775.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>One of his first acts was to summon Goethe to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, and Goethe joined the privy council in 1776. The duke funded the Weimar Free Drawing School, encouraged Weimar Classicism and summoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a> to reform education, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-jena">University of Jena</a> reached the height of its fame. By his own account his aim was to educate his people to independence of judgment. Weimar became the intellectual centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>In the affairs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> he worked for unity. He was the prime mover of the league of princes of 1785, which under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> frustrated the plans of Emperor Joseph II, and he refused the offer of the Hungarian crown. From 1788 he served in the Prussian army, and he was present with Goethe at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-valmy">Battle of Valmy</a> in 1792. After the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> following the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> in 1806 he was forced to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, and his contingent fought on the French side until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> campaign of 1812. In 1813 he joined the Sixth Coalition.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 he protested against the powers' neglect of the rights of the people, and he received more territory and the title of grand duke. On 5 May 1816 he granted his state a liberal constitution under Article XIII of the Act of Confederation, and he allowed liberty of the press. Metternich mocked him for patronising the Burschenschaften, and the festival held at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> with his permission in 1817 angered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, so that the press was restricted again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Karl August died at Graditz, near Torgau, on 14 June 1828. Goethe wrote that he had the gift of discriminating intellects and characters and setting each in its place. His correspondence with Goethe was published at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> in 1863. He left two surviving sons, Karl Frederick, who succeeded him, and Karl Bernhard, a soldier.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karl August, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Duchess Anna Amalia and <b>Charles Augustus</b> the united duchy became the home of Goethe and Schiller.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar</a></li><li><blockquote>Duchess Anna Amalia, regent from 1758, invited Christoph Martin Wieland as tutor; her son <b>Charles Augustus</b> brought Goethe to Weimar in 1775, later followed by Herder and Schiller, while the University of Jena flourished.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar-eisenach">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Graditz, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.98,11.33&amp;year=1803" aria-label="Weimar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4807 396 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles Augustus, Grand Duke of Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5257 666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5257 666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.98,11.33&amp;year=1803">What was here in 1803? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
